Description: CVE-2026-119618 - mod_http2: assign_headers() updates the wrong header table
 In assign_headers() (modules/http2/h2_request.c) the request headers are first
 cloned into r->headers_in. When a request has a Content-Type but the stream has
 already seen EOS (no request body will follow), the code is meant to signal an
 explicit "Content-Length: 0". It wrote that header into req->headers, the
 shared h2_request table, instead of the request's own r->headers_in table.
 Modifying the shared table from the request worker without synchronization is
 the "concurrent execution using shared resource with improper synchronization"
 flaw described in the advisory, which can lead to heap corruption and remote
 code execution; the request itself also never received the intended
 Content-Length: 0. Write the header into r->headers_in instead.
 .
 Focal's apache2 2.4.41 carries mod_http2 2.0.22 via update_http2.patch, which
 is within the affected mod_h2 range (2.0.8 through 2.0.42), so the vulnerable
 line is present and this fix applies unchanged.
 .
 Upstream: apache/httpd commit 824beab9 (2.4.x, fixed in 2.4.71), a cherry-pick
 of trunk commit 5ed39287.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/apache/httpd/commit/824beab9b9424d6cfb29595248fdbf6c3dddeab1
Bug: https://httpd.apache.org/security/vulnerabilities_24.html#CVE-2026-119618
Author: Eric Covener <covener@apache.org>
Last-Update: 2026-11-23
Forwarded: not-needed
---
--- a/modules/http2/h2_request.c
+++ b/modules/http2/h2_request.c
@@ -345,7 +345,7 @@
                 /* If we have a content-type, but already seen eos, no more
                  * data will come. Signal a zero content length explicitly.
                  */
-                apr_table_setn(req->headers, "Content-Length", "0");
+                apr_table_setn(r->headers_in, "Content-Length", "0");
             }
         }
 #if !AP_HAS_RESPONSE_BUCKETS
